1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,850
Welcome to BeAIExpert. In this series, we'll build a RAG application from scratch.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:12,850
Let's start with a problem. Imagine asking an AI assistant a question about your own company.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,850
It answers instantly, and it sounds confident. But the answer is wrong.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,850
Why? The model was never trained on your company's private documents.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,850
And its knowledge is frozen at a training cutoff date, so it can't know anything newer.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,850
When a model lacks information, it doesn't stay silent. It produces a plausible guess.

7
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:40,850
One idea is to paste all your documents into every prompt.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,850
That works for a few pages. But a real company has thousands.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,850
They won't fit in the model's context window. And even when they do...

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,850
...every question becomes slow and expensive, and the important detail gets lost in the noise.

11
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:02,850
Here's a better way to think about it.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,850
A plain language model is like a student taking a closed-book exam: it answers from memory.

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,350
RAG turns it into an open-book exam. First look up the relevant pages, then answer using them.

14
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,850
Retrieval-Augmented Generation: retrieve the right information, then generate the answer.

15
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:24,350
The name describes the three steps.

16
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:28,850
Retrieval: search your documents for the passages that matter for this question.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,350
Augmented: add those passages to the prompt.

18
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:36,850
Generation: the language model writes an answer grounded in them.

19
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:43,350
A RAG system works in two phases. The first is indexing, which you do once, ahead of time.

20
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:47,850
Start with your documents: PDFs, web pages, policies, notes.

21
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,850
Split them into smaller passages, called chunks, so we can find precise pieces later.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,850
Turn each chunk into an embedding: a list of numbers that captures its meaning.

23
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:08,850
Store those embeddings in an index, ready to be searched.

24
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:14,850
Why embeddings? Picture every chunk as a point on a map of meaning.

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,850
Chunks about similar topics end up close together.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,850
When a question arrives, we turn it into a point on the same map...

27
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,850
...and the closest chunks are the most relevant, even if they use completely different words.

28
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,850
That's semantic search, and it's the heart of retrieval.

29
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,850
Phase two happens every time someone asks a question.

30
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,850
We embed the question with the same model, and find the closest chunks in the index.

31
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,850
Then we build a prompt: clear instructions, the retrieved passages, and the question.

32
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,850
Notice the instructions: use only this context, and say "I don't know" if the answer isn't there.

33
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:08,850
Finally, the language model reads it all and writes the answer.

34
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,850
Let's ask the same question again, this time with RAG.

35
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,850
The answer is correct, because it comes from the actual policy document.

36
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,850
And it cites its sources, so anyone can check where the answer came from.

37
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,850
That's the difference between a confident guess and a grounded answer.

38
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,350
So when should you use RAG?

39
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:36,850
When the answers live in private or internal documents...

40
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,850
...when information changes often, or there's far too much to fit in a prompt...

41
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,850
...and whenever answers need sources people can verify.

42
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,850
That's why RAG powers so many real assistants: customer support, internal knowledge bases, and document search.

43
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:56,850
In this series, we'll build a complete RAG application step by step, in Python.

44
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,850
Next, we'll load and chunk documents, then create embeddings and semantic search.

45
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,850
Then we'll retrieve the right passages, and generate grounded answers with citations...

46
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,850
...and finally measure and improve quality, like a real production system.

47
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:17,350
Want to read ahead? The full guide is free at beaiexpert.com.

48
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:21,850
See you in episode two.
